{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And the eyes of Leah were tender, etc.</b> It is not fitting for Scripture to relate a disparagement of the righteous woman, mother of the tribes of Judah, heaven forbid. Rather, its intention was to inform us that there was no possibility for Laban to switch them, for Leah was distinctly different from Rachel: Leah not only was not beautiful, but furthermore her eyes were tender, which is a branch of bodily unattractiveness; and Rachel not only had no unattractiveness, but was beautiful of form and beautiful of appearance. From this it follows that the avenue of deception was locked before Laban, and his deceits were nullified." "<b>Beautiful of form and beautiful of appearance.</b> The meaning is that there are two aspects to beauty: one is in each and every detail, as you would say the eyes, the nose, the cheeks, the neck — and go and learn from what King Solomon specified in the Song of Songs; the second aspect is the overall appearance of the face and the overall appearance of the body. Therefore it says ״יְפַת תֹּאַר וִיפַת מַרְאֶה״ — her details were each beautiful in and of themselves, and also in the way they came together." "<b>Further,</b> it intends <b>beautiful of form</b> according to its plain meaning; <b>and beautiful of appearance</b> — that she had a grace (חֵן) such that everyone who saw her would desire her."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:43.352080931Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}